Confirmed. The failure is fully reproducible. Now the root-cause analysis:

**The `sweep_holds` loop has two intertwined bugs:**

1. **Index-skip-on-erase.** `for (i=0; ...) { ...; queue_.erase(begin()+i); ...; }` then `i++`. After erasing slot `i`, the element at `i+1` slides down into `i`, but the `i++` skips straight past it. Two *adjacent* lapsed holds → the second is never examined. That's exactly why T-103's neighbor T-104 survives the minute-40 sweep, why only 2 of 3 are reported dropped, and why the all-adjacent run `[T-201..T-204]` drops only T-201 and T-203.

2. **Stale `by_ticket_` indices.** The map stores `ticket → vector index`. `queue_.erase(i)` shifts every later element down by one, but the loop only erases the *removed* ticket from the map; the survivors' indices are never rewritten, so they all stay too large. After dropping T-103 at index 2: `by_ticket_["T-104"]` is still `3`, but T-104 now lives at index 2 — so `lookup("T-104")` returns `queue_[3]` = Clover. Meanwhile `by_ticket_["T-105"]=4` is now `>= size()` (4), so `lookup("T-105")` returns `nullptr` even though Clover is sitting at index 3. This hits after even a single early erase — the "later tickets pull up the wrong chart" symptom.

The fix: compact the queue in one stable forward pass with read/write cursors, erase lapsed tickets from the map, and **re-index every survivor** to its new compacted slot. `lookup` stays an O(log n) map probe — no re-scan added.
