Both failures reproduced. Now let me analyze the root cause of each.

**Failure 1 — `rejects malformed prices outright`:** In `parsePriceCents`, the guard is `if (dollars === NaN || ...)`. But `NaN === NaN` is always `false` in JS (NaN is never equal to itself), so `parsePriceCents('n/a')` silently returns `NaN` instead of throwing. The guard exists but is dead.

**Failure 2 — `a line saved for later (quantity 0) costs nothing`:** In `lineTotalCents`, the line is `const quantity = item.quantity || 1;`. Since `0` is falsy, `0 || 1` evaluates to `1`, so a saved-for-later line (quantity 0) gets charged for one unit. Note the very next line already uses the correct idiom for `discountPct ?? 0` — so this is an inconsistency.

